\bibglsnewentry{karambha}%
{sort={an|aroid|},
type={plants}}%
{an aroid}%
{probably a plant belonging to Araceae, \cite[76]{gvdb} for useful discussion. E.g., Alocasia macrorrhiza (L.) G.Don is an Old World aroid occuring in S. Asia and has poisonous sap; any part of the aroid Colocasia spp.\ chewed or eaten raw can cause burning pain and buccal swelling, salivation, difficulty breathing, swallowing or speaking. E.g., C. esculenta (L.) is native to India and has these properties, \cite[1060--62]{WDMPP}.  The same source (2847--2848) gives the extremely irritant Pergularia daemia (Forssk.) Chiov.\ as \emph{karambha}. Cf.\ \egls{piṇḍāluka}   \emph{karambha} is possibly a  syn.\ for \egls{indīvara}, \cite[76, 44--45]{gvdb}}


