\bibglsnewentry{prapauṇḍarīka}%
{sort={climbing|diamond|flower|},
type={plants}}%
{climbing diamond flower}%
{conjectural; see the substantial discussion by \citet[261]{gvdb}.  They note that it is used mainly in eye troubles and frequently with \egls{yaṣṭimadhu}, than which it is has been said to be thicker, and is sweet in taste.  Candidates they suggest include Wendlandia heynei (Schult.) Santapau \& Merchant (formerly W. exserta), native to India and Hedychium flavescens, Carey.  But neither of these plants have any reports of toxicity.  Another Indian candidate may be Hedyotis scandens Roxb. (syn.\ Dimetia scandens (Roxb.) R.J.Wang) that is poisonous but also used for eye conditions and whose root is abortifacient; the whole plant is used as a fish poison \citep[1934]{WDMPP}}


