\bibglsnewentry{rāsnā}%
{location={\glsnoidxdisplayloc{}{page}{glsnumberformat}{17}},
sort={grey|orchid|},
type={plants}}%
{grey orchid}%
{Vanda tessellata (Roxb.) Hook.\ ex G.Don, usually. But Pluchea lanceolata (DC.) C.B.Clarke, is a more common identification in Punjab and Gujarat \citep[337--338]{gvdb}; Alpinia galanga (L.) Sw.\ is more common in Kerala (\cite[398]{adps};  \volcite{2}[303--318]{pete-2012}), though this is usually identified with \egls{galaṅgala}. As all authorities note, the identification of this plant is debated.  \citet[398--401]{adps} note that sources describe it as having leaves like cardamom and sweet-smelling roots and that ``there is great confusion with regard to the identity of the drug rāsnā''}
