\bibglsnewentry{varmimatsya}%
{sort={Indian|mottled|eel|},
type={plants}}%
{Indian mottled eel}%
{Almost certainly the mottled eel.    \cite[962c]{moni-sans} noted that the \emph{varmi} fish “is commonly called \emph{vāmi}.”  The “vam fish,” or “\textbengali{বান মাছ} (\emph{bān māch})” in Bengal, is a marine and freshwater eel, \emph{Anguilla bengalensis}. It is the most    common eel in Indian inland waters and a prized food fish    \citep{froe-2022}.  However, some NIA languages identify the “vam” fish    with the Indian Pike Conger, \emph{Congresox talabonides} (Bleeker)    \citep[235, 236]{talw-1984}}


\bibglsnewentry{sarṣapa}%
{sort={Indian|mustard|},
type={plants}}%
{Indian mustard}%
{Brassica juncea, Czern. \& Coss. See \volcite{1}[301]{avs}, \volcite{1}[\#378]{NK}, \gvdb{426--427}, without suggestion of any poisonous tuber}


\bibglsnewentry{sārṣapa}%
{sort={Indian|mustard|derivative|},
type={plants}}%
{Indian mustard derivative}%
{this would normally mean “derived from \egls{sarṣapa}.” Excessive consumption of mustard oil can be harmful. This seems not to fit in a list of tuber poisons (SS 5.2.11--17).  However, the \emph{Sauśrutanighaṇṭu} (156) gives \emph{rakṣoghnā} as a synonym for \emph{sarṣapā}. This can be \textit{Semecarpus anacardium}, L.f., which has some poisonous parts (``the black fruit is toxic and produces a severe allergic reaction if it is consumed or its resin comes in contact with the skin'' \cite{sema-2010}).  But this is still not a tuber product}


