\bibglsnewentry{tilvaka}%
{sort={viburnum|},
type={plants}}%
{viburnum}%
{Viburnum nervosum D.Don.  In their thoughtful article, \citet[185--186]{gvdb} separate \emph{tilvaka} from \emph{lodhra}, a conflation they attribute to Dṛḍhabala. They identify V. nervosum because of its use under a similar local name in Garhawal and Gangotri and the match with its purging properties mentioned in ayurvedic literature. \volcite{5}[219]{avs} makes the same separation, noting that in Kerala the plant Jatropha curcas L. is used.  But that is a native of the new world.  Cf.\ many Viburnum varieties listed by \citet[1200\,ff.]{IGP}. \cite{kew} confirms that V. nervosum has an appropriate Himalayan distribution.  \emph{Tilvaka} is also sometimes wrongly considered to be a synonym of \egls{tilaka}, \gvdb{185--186}}


