{sometimes $\rightarrow$ \emph{śvetā}, in which case possibly Clitoria ternatea, L., see \volcite{2}[129]{avs},  \volcite{1}[\#621]{NK}. \gvdb{41, 138--139} argued against the identification with C. ternatea L.  Since \emph{śvetā} and \emph{girihvā} are cited as separate constitutents of one formula (e.g., \SS\ \Su{5.5.75}{579} they cannot be the same plant.   \cite[138--139]{gvdb} argued for Symphorema polyandrum Wight, which they also assigned to \emph{sinduvāra}.  When discussing \emph{śaṅkhapuṣpī}, another possible synonym,  \citet[425--427]{adps} also suggest Canscora alata (Roth) Wall. (syn of Canscora decussata Schultes \& Schultes f.) and Convulvulus pluricaulis Chois.  The former has a more appropriate distribution and is chosen here}
